{"number": 103, "text": "                      Exercises\n\n\n              48.1\n                      Match  each idiom on the left with its definition on the right.\n\n                         have itchy feet                                      busy\n                      oO ON DO\n                         find your feet                                start off in a bad way\n                         be under someone’s feet                       feel familiar with something\n                         land/fall on your feet                        be restless / want to travel\n                         get off on the wrong foot                     regret a decision\n                         be rushed off your feet                       remain connected to the real world\n                         get cold feet                                 be constantly in the way\n                         stand on your own two feet                    be lucky/successful\n                                                                    i  beindependent\n                         keep both feet on the ground\n\n                      Write sentences which relate to your life with any five of the idioms in exercise 48.1.\n              48.2\n                      EXAMPLE         for a place at an American university and was accepted, but then | ‘got cold feet.\n              48.3\n                      Rewrite these sentences  with five of the idioms from exercise 48.1.\n                      1  | said | would join Tom on the protest march, but then regretted it and didn’t go at all.\n                      2  She was very busy in the shop last month, but she’s pleased that the business is doing well.\n                      3      have to learn to make his own decisions now that he’s at college and not living at home\n                         any more.\n                      4  Rosie and | started off rather badly when she first joined the company, but now we’re working\n                         very well together.\n                      5     feeling restless these days. I’d love to go off on a backpacking holiday somewhere.\n\n              48.4\n                      Which  idioms do these pictures make you think of?\n                      1 |                         may 2\n\n\n              48.5\n                      True or false? Tick (/) the correct box.                                  True        False\n                      1  If you drag your heels, you deliberately act slowly or delay something.\n                      2  If you put your foot down, you tell someone very firmly to act in\n                         a particular way.                                                                   L)\n                      3  If someone keeps you on your toes, they keep you very excited.          O\n                      4   you follow in someone’s footsteps, they are your boss and you\n                         are below them.                                                                     LJ\n                      5   you dig your heels in, you are very determined not to be persuaded\n                         to do something you don’t want to do.                                   O\n                      [Over      to to you you\n                         Use a good dictionary to find the meanings of these idioms if you do not already know them.\n                         foot the bill\n                      _  toe the line\n                         hard/hot on the heels of                                                                 J\n\n                                                                           English Idioms in Use Intermediate     101", "char_count": 3641, "mean_conf": 89.4, "word_count": 410, "words": [{"text": "48.1", "x0": 284, "y0": 376, "x1": 388, "y1": 413, "conf": 95, "bold": true}, {"text": "48.2", "x0": 284, "y0": 1157, "x1": 389, "y1": 1195, "conf": 95, "bold": true}, {"text": "48.3", "x0": 281, "y0":